      "dom_id": "question-78201",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)=-7{x}^{3}$, what is ${f}^{′}(x)$ according to the definition of the derivative?",
      "worked_solution": "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=-7{x}^{3}$,  we have that $f(x+h)=-7(x+h)^{3}.$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-7(x+h)^{3}-(-7{x}^{3})}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-7(x+h)^{3}+7{x}^{3}}{h}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
